-- ============================================================================
-- payer-rules-oh-appendixdd-delisted.sql
--
-- Ohio DELETED 30 codes from Appendix DD. 150 live rules across the five
-- Ohio Medicaid plans still cite those rows, and 0 of them say "covered".
--
-- HOW THE DELETION WAS ESTABLISHED, because the obvious test gets it wrong
-- Searching the .xlsx for the code text finds it — G0318 is right there in
-- xl/sharedStrings.xml. It is a GHOST. Excel does not garbage-collect the
-- shared-string table when rows are deleted, so 734 of that workbook's 8,358
-- strings are orphans left behind by removed rows. Only resolving cell
-- references shows what the sheet actually contains, and no cell references
-- any of these codes.
--
-- So a naive "is this code in the file?" check says the citation is fine. It
-- is not. The row is gone.
--
-- WHY 'unknown' AND NOT 'not_covered'
-- The workbook does say "Unless otherwise indicated, services and items
-- represented by codes in the following ranges are not covered." — but that
-- governs the RANGES it then enumerates, not any absent code. I cannot show
-- these codes fall inside them, so asserting non-coverage would be inventing
-- a determination Ohio has not published. Absent is not denied; it is
-- unpublished. Same reading as payer-rules-sc-carecoord-absent.sql.
--
-- G0318 matters most here: prolonged home E/M, one of the 25 palliative codes,
-- marked COVERED for all five Ohio plans on the strength of a row that no
-- longer exists. That is the same defect as the seven hand-typed rules
-- withdrawn in d1ba7a5 — a coverage claim with no document behind it —
-- reached by a different route.
--
-- Each absence is proved by the two codes that bracket it in the CURRENT
-- workbook, showing the schedule runs straight past.
-- ============================================================================

BEGIN;

